# Games and trials against computer pilots (domains/recreation). The opponents fly the same
# T1-S on the same physics as the pilot's plane. In the tests, a stand-in pilot (the same
# computer pilot) flies the pilot's sticks for the steps that need flying: a lap, a cut, a
# tag. See src/acceptance.rs.

Section: Recreation
  Fly games against three [[computer pilots]]. Each one flies a T1-S, on the same physics as
  your plane. Press G for a [[pylon race]]. Press T for a game of [[tag]]. Press the same key
  again to stop the game and fly freely.

  A game starts with all the planes in the hands of their pilots, at the [[pilot box]]. The
  game panel on the right of the view counts down to GO. Then the computer pilots throw their
  planes, and you throw yours. Labels on the view show the name of each opponent above its
  plane.

Vocabulary:
  State: the opponents are in hand | on the ground | launching | flying | down
    Shows: in hand: the three computer pilots hold their planes beside you
    Shows: flying: the computer pilots throw their planes, one each second, and the planes climb away
  State: the lap count is 0..5
    Shows: the game panel says LAP {value}/5
  State: the cut count is 0..2
    Shows: the game panel says CUTS {value}
  State: the chaser is you | an opponent
    Shows: you: the game panel says YOU ARE IT
    Shows: an opponent: the game panel says IT and the name of the opponent, and the label above its plane turns red

  Event: the pilot starts a race
    Say: press G
  Event: the pilot starts a game of tag
    Say: press T
  Event: the pilot ends the game
    Say: press G again to stop a race, or T again to stop a game of tag
  Event: the countdown ends
  Event: the pilot flies a lap
    Say: fly round the outside of pylon 1, pylon 2 and pylon 3. Then fly across the start line
  Event: the pilot cuts a pylon
    Say: turn inside the next pylon
  Event: the pilot tags an opponent
    Say: fly at an opponent until your plane touches it
  Event: the chaser tags the plane
  Event: the pilot leaves the arena
    Say: fly out past the edge of the arena
  Event: the clock runs out

  Phrase: the pilot flies the race
    Say: fly five laps of the course. Keep outside the pylons
    When the pilot flies a lap
    When the pilot flies a lap
    When the pilot flies a lap
    When the pilot flies a lap
    When the pilot flies a lap
